          "type": "concept",
          "title": "2. Word equations and chemical equations",
          "text": "A word equation names the reacting substances and products in words. A chemical equation replaces those names with chemical formulae. Reactants are written on the left, an arrow separates reactants from products, and products appear on the right. State symbols improve precision: (s) for solid, (l) for liquid, (g) for gas and (aq) for aqueous solution. Conditions such as heat, sunlight or electrolysis are conventionally written above the reaction arrow."
